Exercise 5-30E: effect of raising soil conductivity from 0.49 to 1.50 Btu/(h·ft·°F)

I re-solved both cases with the same code and the same node layout. All numbers below come from that solver, and I hand-checked them with a resistance model. 1.50 is treated as a hypothetical design value, not a verified property.

Verification against the image and my first solution
Node layout confirmed: nodes 0–5 are in the steel (5 intervals of 1 in, node 5 at the interface). Nodes 5–10 are in the soil (5 intervals of 0.6 ft). Node 10 sits at 3 ft depth and is fixed at 50°F.
Discrepancy to flag: in the first answer’s radiation line I wrote a garbled expression (“0.6(0.1714×10⁻⁹·10)... in full:”). Please ignore that fragment. The correct term is 0.6(0.1714×10⁻⁸)(510⁴ − (T₀+459.67)⁴), which gives −14.32 Btu/h·ft². The numerical results in that answer were unaffected.
Same assumptions as before: σ = 0.1714×10⁻⁸ Btu/h·ft²·R⁴ and °R = °F + 459.67. Using 460 instead shifts temperatures by about 0.05°F.
1. Physical reasoning

Heat enters the plate from the 80°F air (convection) and is partly lost to the cold sky (radiation). The net gain is conducted through the steel and then the soil to the 50°F boundary at 3 ft.

The steel and the soil are thermal resistances in series, and the soil dominates: its resistance is about 99% of the total. The steel is thin and conductive and contributes almost nothing.

Raising k_soil shrinks the dominant resistance. The plate surface can then sit closer to the 50°F sink, so more heat is drawn in from the air (convection increases) and less is lost to the sky (radiation decreases).

2. Thermal resistances (per 1 ft², h·ft²·°F/Btu)

R = L/k

Quantity	Original	Modified
R_steel = (5/12 ft)/7.2	0.05787	0.05787
R_soil = 3 ft/k_soil	3/0.49 = 6.1224	3/1.5 = 2.0000
R_total (T₀ to 50°F)	6.1803	2.0579

The soil’s share of R_total drops from 99.1% to 97.2%.

3. Finite difference equations (all 11 nodes)

Let Δx_s = 1/12 ft and Δx_g = 0.6 ft. Then k/Δx_s = 86.4 Btu/h·ft²·°F.

Node 0 (nonlinear): h(T∞ − T₀) + εσ[T_sky⁴ − (T₀+459.67)⁴] + 86.4(T₁ − T₀) = 0
Nodes 1–4 (steel): Tₘ₋₁ − 2Tₘ + Tₘ₊₁ = 0
Node 5 (interface): see section 4.
Nodes 6–9 (soil): Tₘ₋₁ − 2Tₘ + Tₘ₊₁ = 0
Node 10: T₁₀ = 50°F
4. Interface equation at node 5

Take a control volume around node 5. It is half steel (thickness Δx_s/2) and half soil (thickness Δx_g/2), with area A.

Steady state, no generation and no storage give:

Q̇ from steel + Q̇ from soil = 0

k·A(T₄ − T₅)/Δx_s + k_soil·A(T₆ − T₅)/Δx_g = 0

Unequal properties and spacing. Each conduction term uses its own conductivity and its own distance to the neighboring node. The half-volume sizes multiply only generation and storage terms, which are zero here, so they cancel. No interpolation or averaging of k is needed.

Dividing by A and inserting numbers:

Original: 86.4(T₄ − T₅) + 0.81667(T₆ − T₅) = 0, i.e. 86.4T₄ − 87.2167T₅ + 0.81667T₆ = 0
Modified (k_soil/Δx_g = 1.5/0.6 = 2.5): 86.4(T₄ − T₅) + 2.5(T₆ − T₅) = 0, i.e. 86.4T₄ − 88.9T₅ + 2.5T₆ = 0

The steel-to-soil conductance ratio falls from about 106 to about 35.

5. Node 0 boundary condition (modified case)

The surface half-cell receives convection and radiation from outside and conduction from node 1. The equation is the same as above. Only T₀ appears raised to the fourth power, and it must be in °R. This is the only nonlinear equation, so I solved it with a Newton-type solver.

